{"parentUuid":"287b9224-75ef-4441-9776-18a7bbad878f","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4f47bc86-42e6-4104-bc35-aaaefe8f740b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"而且我发现录出来的只要一点鼠标的时候有一秒是色彩恢复正常，然后就又白了"},"uuid":"1e48dee5-f50d-47d3-9b79-33809e3f8a6c","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:30:06.987Z","permissionMode":"default","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\13751","sessionId":"7ad04b1c-b250-4929-92e2-5d7da59611e9","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"1e48dee5-f50d-47d3-9b79-33809e3f8a6c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"b864e944-92c1-474d-aeed-16e57a702793","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a very interesting clue. The user says that when they click the mouse, the colors become normal for about one second, then go back to being washed out/overexposed.\n\nThis pattern - \"click makes it normal for a second, then goes white again\" - is a classic symptom of HDR being enabled on the monitor. Here's what's happening:\n\n1. When the user is not interacting, Windows sees a static HDR game/desktop and OBS captures the HDR signal but maps it to SDR incorrectly → overexposed\n2. When the user clicks the mouse, Windows triggers an SDR/HDR switching behavior - the click might be on an SDR window or trigger a brief mode shift, making OBS show the correct SDR for a moment\n3. After ~1 second, Windows/HDR kicks back in and it goes white again\n\nOR more likely:\n- The game capture or display capture has some kind of auto-HDR or HDR capture behavior where clicking (activating a window, focusing) briefly resets the capture to SDR before HDR kicks back in.\n\nBut actually, let me think about this more carefully. The OBS settings show:\n- ColorFormat=NV12\n- ColorSpace=709\n- ColorRange=Partial\n- SdrWhiteLevel=300\n- HdrNominalPeakLevel=1000\n\nThe `SdrWhiteLevel=300` and `HdrNominalPeakLevel=1000` settings suggest OBS is aware of HDR content. But ColorSpace is set to 709 (SDR), not Rec.2100 (HDR).\n\nThe \"click fixes it for 1 second\" behavior is very telling. This sounds like:\n\n**Windows HDR is enabled on the monitor, and when you click (interact with a game/window), Windows momentarily switches the display mode or the game briefly renders in SDR before the HDR pipeline kicks back in.**\n\nOr alternatively:\n- The game being captured runs in HDR mode\n- OBS game capture hooks into the game\n- When clicking, there's a brief moment where the capture pipeline resets and shows the raw SDR frame\n- Then HDR tonemapping kicks in and makes it white again\n\nThe real fix here is to either:\n1. Turn off HDR in Windows display settings\n2. Or configure OBS to properly handle HDR → SDR conversion\n\nLet me check - the user's OBS is using `h264_texture_amf` (AMD encoder). The encoder itself shouldn't cause this issue.\n\nThe key diagnostic: **\"Click makes it normal for 1 second then white again\"** = almost certainly an HDR-related issue. The brief normalization happens when the GPU/compositor briefly switches out of HDR mode during the click interaction.\n\nLet me tell the user to check Windows HDR settings and suggest they either:\n1. Turn off Windows HDR\n2. Or if they need HDR, set OBS up properly for HDR capture\n\nActually, I should also look at whether the display being captured is an HDR monitor. The monitor ID in the scene config is `AUS2581` which is likely an ASUS monitor. Let me check - `AUS2581` is likely an ASUS VG258 or similar. \n\nThe most actionable advice: check Windows HDR setting first. That matches the symptom perfectly.","signature":"b864e944-92c1-474d-aeed-16e57a702793"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":85,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46208,"output_tokens":1114,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"e890ba68-1150-4454-a31a-7d83ece5e808","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:30:34.147Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\13751","sessionId":"7ad04b1c-b250-4929-92e2-5d7da59611e9","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"e890ba68-1150-4454-a31a-7d83ece5e808","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"b864e944-92c1-474d-aeed-16e57a702793","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"text","text":"这就基本确诊了。\n\n### 这个症状是 HDR 的典型表现\n\n「点鼠标正常一秒然后变白」= Windows HDR 在搞鬼。 过程是这样的：\n\n1. 桌面/游戏运行在 HDR 模式 → OBS 按 SDR 捕获 HDR 画面 → 颜色全部错误 → 过曝泛白\n2. **你点鼠标的瞬间**，Windows 触发了一个短暂的显示模式切换（比如焦点窗口变化，或者 GPU 刷新捕获缓冲区），这一瞬间 OBS 拿到了未映射的原始帧 → 颜色正常\n3. 大约 1 秒后 HDR 管线恢复 → 又白了\n\n### 立刻检查这个\n\n**Win + I** → 系统 → 屏幕 → HDR（或者直接按 **Win + Alt + B** 切换 HDR）：\n\n看看「使用 HDR」是不是**开着**。\n\n### 解决（二选一）\n\n**方案 A — 关掉 Windows HDR（简单粗暴）：**\n- 设置 → 系统 → 屏幕 → HDR → 关掉\n- 然后 OBS 的色彩空间 Rec.709 + Partial 就是对的\n- 再把之前那堆滤镜的 Gamma 改回 0\n- 录出来直接就是正常颜色\n\n**方案 B — 保留 HDR，OBS 正确处理：**\n- OBS → 设置 → 高级 → 视频：\n  - 色彩格式：`P010 (10-bit)`\n  - 色彩空间：`Rec.2100 (PQ)`\n  - 范围：`Partial`\n- 然后在「游戏采集」源上加一个 HDR 色调映射滤镜（OBS 29.1+ 内置）\n\n**方案 A 省事，方案 B 保留 HDR 观感但多一步处理。** 你先 **Win + Alt + B** 试试关掉 HDR 录一段，大概率直接好。"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":85,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":46208,"output_tokens":1114,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"6b7386b0-2836-4157-9d5f-6d061e2aa8ef","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:30:48.880Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\13751","sessionId":"7ad04b1c-b250-4929-92e2-5d7da59611e9","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
