{"parentUuid":"9777ca32-da0e-4791-a938-614cf0a27864","isSidechain":false,"message":{"id":"9ee8ed9e-d40a-4408-980d-5627c2302254","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key findings:\n\n1. **DisplayTransferCharacteristic: 120** — This is critical! In EDID specs, transfer characteristic 120 is \"sRGB\" which is... let me think. Actually, the EDID transfer characteristic values are:\n   - 0 = undefined\n   - 1 = bt.709\n   - 2 = bt.470/bt.601\n   - 4 = gamma 2.2\n   - 12 = PQ (HDR)\n   - 13 = HLG (HDR)\n   - 16 = sRGB\n   - 17 = bt.2020\n\nWait, 120 is not a standard value. Let me re-check. The WMI `DisplayTransferCharacteristic` values... Actually it might be using different encoding. Let me look at this differently.\n\nActually `120` in hex is `0x78`. In the EDID specification, the transfer characteristic byte has values where:\n- Bits 0-7 can encode different gamma/transfer functions\n\nActually, I think the WMI value of 120 might correspond to the EDID byte 0x78 which could be... This is confusing. Let me think about the actual monitor.\n\nThe monitor is `AUS2581` which is likely an ASUS monitor (ASUS vendor ID is AUS). The model ID 2581... Let me look this up.\n\nActually, let me focus on what matters. The key new finding is in the **recordEncoder.json**:\n\n```json\n{\"rate_control\":\"CBR\",\"cqp\":23,\"keyint_sec\":0,\"bf\":2,\"bitrate\":50000,\"pre_analysis\":false}\n```\n\nThe encoder is `h264_texture_amf` (from basic.ini), which is AMD's AMF hardware encoder. The bitrate is 50000 kbps (50 Mbps), which is quite high.\n\nNow, the most significant finding is the encoder: **h264_texture_amf (AMD AMF)**. AMD's encoder has known quirks with color space handling, especially:\n1. AMF can sometimes apply its own color space conversion\n2. Full/Partial color range mismatches between what OBS tells AMF and what AMF actually outputs\n3. There could be VCN (Video Core Next) driver-level color enhancement settings\n\nBut wait - the user's symptom is very specific: \"when I click the mouse, it's normal for 1 second, then white again\". This really sounds like:\n1. The game or display briefly switches to SDR mode when clicking\n2. OR there's a focus issue where the game capture method changes between fullscreen and windowed mode\n3. OR the game itself has some adaptive HDR feature\n\nLet me also check:\n- Does the game_capture use \"any_fullscreen\" which could have issues?\n- The monitor is AUS2581 - what monitor is that?\n- Are there AMD driver color enhancement settings active?\n\nLet me look at the AMD driver settings and also check what monitor this is. Also check if there's actual HDR in the Windows display settings.\n\nActually, the HDR registry key didn't return anything, which suggests HDR might not be configured via that path in newer Windows. Let me check differently.\n\nActually, let me try to get the display's actual HDR state through Windows Settings API, and also look up what monitor AUS2581 is.\n\nAlso critical: `DisplayTransferCharacteristic: 120` - Let me look at this more carefully. The WMI `WmiMonitorBasicDisplayParams` class's `DisplayTransferCharacteristic` uses VESA DisplayID values, not EDID values. In VESA DisplayID 2.0:\n- 0x78 = 120 decimal could be... Let me think. \n\nActually, I just realized: 120 in the WMI `DisplayTransferCharacteristic` field could just be the raw gamma value × 100. So 120 = gamma 1.2? That seems too low. Or it could be the EDID byte directly.\n\nEDID byte 0x11 (transfer characteristic):\n- 0x00 = undefined\n- 0x01 = BT.709\n- 0x02 = BT.470 (sRGB gamma)\n- 0x03 = BT.470\n- 0x04 = Gamma 2.2\n- 0x05 = Gamma 2.8\n\nSo 120 (0x78) doesn't match the standard EDID. Hmm. The WMI field might use a different encoding.\n\nOK, let me focus on actionable things. I should:\n1. Check the monitor model\n2. Check AMD driver color settings \n3. Check if the game itself might be outputting HDR\n4. Check the OBS game capture settings more carefully\n\nThe encoder is AMF with `h264_texture_amf`, and the record encoder shows `cqp: 23` which is a quality setting. But more importantly, AMF encoder can have issues with color range.\n\nActually, let me just get more info on the setup and present findings clearly. I'm spending too much time on the EDID decoding.\n\nWait - I just realized something. The `basic.ini` shows `[AdvOut]` section with `Encoder=h264_texture_amf` and `RecEncoder=h264_texture_amf`. But this is the STREAM encoder field. The recording encoder is `RecEncoder=h264_texture_amf`.\n\nAnd in the recordEncoder.json: `\"rate_control\":\"CBR\",\"cqp\":23` — this is a bit odd. CBR and CQP are different rate control methods. CBR = Constant Bitrate, CQP = Constant Quantization Parameter. Having both specified is unusual.\n\nLet me also check: does the user have the AMD Adrenalin software settings with video color enhancement features turned on? Let me check the registry for AMD settings.\n\nLet me get the monitor info and AMD driver settings.","signature":"9ee8ed9e-d40a-4408-980d-5627c2302254"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":526,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":47360,"output_tokens":1792,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"stop_details":null},"type":"assistant","uuid":"f9b5a303-c694-4b41-9b3f-b502e61816f5","timestamp":"2026-05-19T15:53:20.980Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"C:\\Users\\13751","sessionId":"7ad04b1c-b250-4929-92e2-5d7da59611e9","version":"2.1.142","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
